<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hendrik Witbooi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1830–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as !Nanseb ǀGabemab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hendrik Witbooi was the leader of the Witbooi Nama of Gibeon in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and the most persistent opponent of German colonial rule there. After resisting the Germans in the 1890s he signed a protection treaty in 1894, but in 1904 he rose against them. He was killed in battle in 1905 and is a national hero of Namibia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hendrik Witbooi</b>, captain of the ǀKhowesin, was killed fighting the Germans in 1905.</blockquote><a href="/culture/khoekhoe">Khoekhoe · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Herero rising of 1904 and the Nama war under <b>Hendrik Witbooi</b> were crushed by General Lothar von Trotha; tens of thousands died in the desert and in concentration camps such as Shark Island.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Hendrik Witbooi</b>, grandson of Kido, saw himself as a Christian leader with a divine mission and fought long campaigns against the Herero for cattle and land.</blockquote><a href="/state/witbooi-nama">Witbooi Nama</a></li></ul></section>
